<main><section class="page" id="p1"><div class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">Your mission begins here · Science reader 01</div><h1>MARS<br/>THE RED PLANET</h1><p>A freezing world. Giant volcanoes. Rust-colored dust. And one enormous mystery: <b>Was Mars ever home to life?</b></p></div></div><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">01 / Discover Mars</div><h2>Imagine standing on another planet.</h2><p class="lead">Red dust covers the ground. Rocky hills stretch into the distance. The sky looks dusty and pink. It is freezing cold, and there is almost no air you can breathe. <b>Welcome to Mars!</b></p><p>Mars is the fourth planet from the Sun. It is about half the size of Earth. Yet it has huge volcanoes, deep valleys, and powerful dust storms. Scientists study Mars to learn what it was like billions of years ago.</p><div class="callout"><strong>BIG QUESTION</strong><br/>What clues might show that water—or even tiny living things—once existed on Mars?</div></div><div class="footer"><span>Perseverance on the floor of Jezero Crater · NASA/JPL-Caltech · PIA24744</span><span class="number">01 / 04</span></div></section>
<section class="page" id="p2"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">02 / Explore the planet</div><h2>Why is Mars red?</h2><div class="grid"><div><p>The rocks and dust on Mars contain <b>iron</b>, a metal also found on Earth. When iron reacts with oxygen, it can form <b>rust</b>.</p><p>You may have seen reddish-brown rust on an old bicycle. A similar chemical change has happened to minerals on Mars. Fine rusty dust covers much of the planet's <b>surface</b>.</p><p>Strong winds can lift this dust into the sky. Sometimes dust storms spread across enormous parts of the planet.</p></div><div><img alt="Jezero Crater photographed by Perseverance navigation cameras, July 3, 2021." class="photo photoTall" data-asset="mars-landscape.jpg" data-nasa-photo="true" data-photo-id="PIA24744" loading="lazy" onerror="this.style.display='none';var f=this.nextElementSibling;if(f)f.hidden=false" src="assets/images/mars-landscape.jpg"/><div class="imageUnavailable" hidden=""><p>Verified NASA photograph not installed yet — no substitute image is shown.</p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/the-end-of-one-drive-by-perseverance-on-the-floor-of-jezero-crater/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Open the original NASA photograph and download its JPEG</a></div><div class="caption">Perseverance saw these rocks and tracks on Jezero Crater’s floor. Contrast enhanced. Credit: NASA/JPL-Caltech. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/the-end-of-one-drive-by-perseverance-on-the-floor-of-jezero-crater/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NASA PIA24744 source</a></div></div></div><div class="divider"></div><h2>A world of giants</h2><p>Mars has the largest known volcano in the solar system: <b>Olympus Mons</b>. It rises about 22 kilometers above the surrounding plains. Mars also has <b>Valles Marineris</b>, an enormous canyon system almost 4,000 kilometers long.</p><div class="callout"><b>WOW FACT</b> · One day on Mars lasts about <b>24 hours and 39 minutes</b>—almost the same as a day on Earth!</div><div aria-label="Scale comparison of Earth and Mars by diameter. Earth is approximately 12,742 kilometers wide; Mars 6,779 kilometers, about 53 percent as wide." class="comparison" role="img"><div><div class="earth planet"></div><div class="sizeNote">EARTH</div></div><div><div class="mars planet"></div><div class="sizeNote">MARS</div></div><div style="font-size:15px;max-width:210px"><strong>Size comparison</strong><br/>Mars is about half as wide as Earth.<br/><span class="small">Circles approximate relative diameter.</span></div></div></div><div class="footer"><span>Science facts: NASA Mars Facts · Images: NASA</span><span class="number">02 / 04</span></div></section>
<section class="page" id="p3"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">03 / Follow the evidence</div><h2>The mystery of ancient water</h2><p class="lead">Today, Mars is a cold, dry desert. But long ago, it may have looked very different.</p><img alt="Perseverance photographed this ancient river delta in June 2022. Colors are enhanced to show differences in the rocks." class="photo" data-asset="mars-delta.jpg" data-nasa-photo="true" data-photo-id="PIA24921" loading="lazy" onerror="this.style.display='none';var f=this.nextElementSibling;if(f)f.hidden=false" src="assets/images/mars-delta.jpg" style="height:340px"/><div class="imageUnavailable" hidden=""><p>Verified NASA photograph not installed yet — no substitute image is shown.</p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/detailed-panorama-of-mars-jezero-crater-delta/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Open the original NASA photograph and download its JPEG</a></div><div class="caption">Perseverance photographed this ancient river delta in June 2022. Colors are enhanced to show differences in the rocks. Credit: NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU/MSSS. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/detailed-panorama-of-mars-jezero-crater-delta/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NASA PIA24921 source</a></div><p>Spacecraft have seen channels that look like ancient riverbeds. Scientists have also found rocks and minerals that formed in the presence of water. These are important pieces of <b>evidence</b>—clues that help us understand the past.</p><p>On Earth, every known living thing needs water. So scientists ask: <b>If Mars once had rivers and lakes, could tiny living things have existed there?</b></p><div class="callout"><strong>SCIENTIST'S NOTE</strong><br/>Scientists have found evidence of ancient water on Mars. They have <b>not confirmed</b> that life ever existed there.</div><h3>Could humans live there today?</h3><p>Mars has a very thin <b>atmosphere</b> made mostly of carbon dioxide. Humans cannot breathe it. Mars is also extremely cold. People would need spacesuits, shelters, air, water, and equipment to survive.</p></div><div class="footer"><span>Evidence summary: NASA Mars and Perseverance missions</span><span class="number">03 / 04</span></div></section>
<section class="page" id="p4"><div class="content"><div class="eyebrow">04 / Join the mission</div><h2>Meet the robot explorers!</h2><div class="grid"><div><p>Humans have not walked on Mars, but robots have! A robot that drives across another planet is called a <b>rover</b>.</p><p>NASA's <b>Perseverance</b> rover landed in 2021. It takes photographs, studies rocks, and collects rock samples. It explores Jezero Crater, which held a lake billions of years ago.</p><p>Every rock may contain a clue about Mars's past.</p></div><div><img alt="Perseverance took this selfie with the Ingenuity helicopter on April 6, 2021. It combines 62 images." class="photo photoTall" data-asset="perseverance-selfie.jpg" data-nasa-photo="true" data-photo-id="PIA24542" loading="lazy" onerror="this.style.display='none';var f=this.nextElementSibling;if(f)f.hidden=false" src="assets/images/perseverance-selfie.jpg"/><div class="imageUnavailable" hidden=""><p>Verified NASA photograph not installed yet — no substitute image is shown.</p><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverances-selfie-with-ingenuity/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Open the original NASA photograph and download its JPEG</a></div><div class="caption">Perseverance took this selfie with the Ingenuity helicopter on April 6, 2021. It combines 62 images. Credit: NASA/JPL-Caltech/MSSS. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverances-selfie-with-ingenuity/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">NASA PIA24542 source</a></div></div></div><h3>5 mission words</h3><div class="words"><div class="word"><b>surface</b>Outside or top part</div><div class="word"><b>iron</b>A metal in rocks</div><div class="word"><b>atmosphere</b>Gases around a planet</div><div class="word"><b>evidence</b>Clues that support an idea</div><div class="word"><b>rover</b>A robot that drives on a world</div></div><div class="divider"></div><h3>Think like a scientist</h3><div class="question">1. Why is Mars red?</div><div class="question">2. Name one way Mars and Earth are alike and one way they are different.</div><div class="question">3. What evidence suggests Mars once had water?</div><div class="question">4. What would you send a rover to investigate? Why?</div><p class="frames"><b>Sentence frames:</b> Mars is red because ____. One clue that water existed is ____. I would investigate ____ because ____.</p><div class="sourcebox"><strong>Sources &amp; photo credits</strong><br/><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/">NASA · Mars Facts</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/perseverances-selfie-with-ingenuity/">NASA · Perseverance selfie</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-end-of-one-drive-by-perseverance-on-the-floor-of-jezero-crater/">NASA · Jezero image</a> · <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/detailed-panorama-of-mars-jezero-crater-delta/">NASA · Delta panorama</a>.<br/><small>NASA source links open online; photographs are stored with this reader.</small></div></div><div class="footer"><span>Grade 5 Science · Space Explorer</span><span class="number">04 / 04</span></div></section>
